# x402-jcs: JSON Canonicalization (RFC 8785), with the ledger's strict statement form.
# Extracted by hand (graftfork roadmap 3.1, the free first step) from
# github.com/craigm26/x402-solidarity at d6653c7, src/solidarity/jcs.ts (79 lines; Apache-2.0,
# Copyright craigm26). Written from what the module is meant to do: its doc comments, its
# callers (src/solidarity/ledger.ts, policy.ts, facilitator.ts), its tests' stated intent, and
# RFC 8785 and RFC 7493, which it cites. No expected value here was copied from running it.
# Every requirement, decision, property and example is a claim a person has not yet accepted:
# jcs.graft (Graft's claims block, roadmap 3.0) marks each one inferred, since duramen 0.3.0
# gives a status to decisions only; CLAIMS.md says where each came from.
# The oracle (oracle.mjs) is written from RFC 8785, not ported from jcs.ts.

duramen 0.2

spec x402-jcs 0.1.0-extracted
  title "JSON Canonicalization Scheme (RFC 8785) with a strict statement form"
  text
    `x402-jcs` writes a JSON value as its one canonical text under RFC 8785, the JSON
    Canonicalization Scheme, so that two parties that hold the same value produce the same bytes
    to hash or sign. It has a strict mode, the form of the solidarity ledger's signed statements,
    which accepts only strings, non-negative integers below 2^53, arrays, objects and null. It
    also answers whether a given text is already exactly the canonical form of the value it
    holds.

    "Number" means an IEEE 754 binary64 value. A JSON number in a request is read as the binary64
    value nearest to it, however it is written.

oracle node oracle-driver.mjs
  source oracle.mjs, oracle-driver.mjs

edge json/rfc8785 via canonicalize value
  decision D-001
edge number-text/ecmascript via canonicalize value
  decision D-001

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
section RQ "Requests"

errors
  bad_request when the line is not a JSON object, or `id` is missing or not a string (the
    response then has `"id": null`)
  unknown_op when `op` is missing, not a string, or not one of the operations
  bad_request when `input` is missing or not an object, a required input field is missing, or
    an input field has the wrong JSON type (`strict` holding `"yes"`, for example)
  no_canonical_form when the value has no canonical form (REQ-JC-003, REQ-JC-008)

req IF-001 "Requests that cannot be handled"
  decision D-006
  text
    A request line that cannot be handled MUST get exactly `{"id": <id>, "error": <code>}`, with
    the code given by the numbered list under Errors, and the driver MUST then continue with the
    next line.
  example raw "{not json"
    expect id = null
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example canonicalize {"value": 1}
    omit id
    expect id = null
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example canonicalise {"value": 1}
    expect error = "unknown_op"
  example canonicalize
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example canonicalize {}
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example isCanonical {"text": 1}
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example canonicalize {"value": 1, "strict": "yes"}
    expect error = "bad_request"

req IF-002 "Extra members"
  text
    Unknown extra members in a request or in `input` MUST be ignored.
  example raw '{"id":"extra","op":"canonicalize","input":{"value":[1],"note":"x"},"trace":true}'
    expect result = "[1]"

open IF-003 "Error text and standard error"
  text
    Any human-readable error text, and what the driver writes to standard error.

open IF-004 "Repeated member names in a request"
  text
    Which member wins when a request object, or a `value` inside one, repeats a member name.
    I-JSON forbids repeated names; the library receives values that are already parsed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
section JC "Canonical text"
  text
    RFC 8785 section 3.2: the canonical text has no whitespace outside strings, writes literals,
    strings and numbers as ECMAScript's JSON.stringify does, and orders object members by name.

op canonicalize
  input value any, strict? boolean
  returns string
  result the canonical text of `value`, as a string; with `strict` true, the statement form

op canonicalBytes
  input value any, strict? boolean
  returns string matching "([0-9a-f][0-9a-f])*"
  result the UTF-8 bytes of the canonical text of `value`, as lowercase hex, two digits a byte

op isCanonical
  input text string, strict? boolean
  returns boolean
  result whether `text` is exactly the canonical text of the JSON value it holds

req JC-001 "Literals"
  decision D-001
  text
    `null`, `true` and `false` MUST be written as those words.
  example canonicalize {"value": null}
    expect result = "null"
  example canonicalize {"value": [true, false, null]}
    expect result = "[true,false,null]"

req JC-002 "Strings"
  decision D-001
  text
    A string MUST be written as `"`, then each UTF-16 code unit as follows, then `"`: `"` and
    `\` with a backslash before them; U+0008, U+0009, U+000A, U+000C and U+000D as `\b`, `\t`,
    `\n`, `\f` and `\r`; any other code unit below U+0020 as `\u` and four lowercase hex digits;
    everything else, including `/`, U+007F, U+2028 and characters outside the Basic
    Multilingual Plane, as itself.
  example canonicalize {"value": "\u0008\u0009\u000a\u000c\u000d"}
    expect result = "\"\\b\\t\\n\\f\\r\""
  example canonicalize {"value": "\u0000\u000b\u001F"}
    expect result = "\"\\u0000\\u000b\\u001f\""
  example canonicalize {"value": "a\"b\\c\/d"}
    expect result = "\"a\\\"b\\\\c/d\""
  example canonicalize {"value": "\u007f\u2028\u00e9\u20ac\ud83d\ude00"}
    expect result = "\"\u007f\u2028\u00e9\u20ac\ud83d\ude00\""

req JC-003 "Unpaired surrogates have no canonical form"
  decision D-002
  text
    A value MUST be refused with `no_canonical_form` when any string in it, a member name
    included, holds a surrogate code unit that is not part of a valid pair (a high surrogate
    followed by a low one). A valid pair is written as itself.
  example canonicalize {"value": "\ud800"}
    expect error = "no_canonical_form"
  example canonicalize {"value": "x\udc00"}
    expect error = "no_canonical_form"
  example canonicalize {"value": "\ude00\ud83d"}
    expect error = "no_canonical_form"
  example canonicalize {"value": ["ok", {"\udc00": 1}]}
    expect error = "no_canonical_form"
  example canonicalBytes {"value": "\ud83d"}
    expect error = "no_canonical_form"
  example canonicalize {"value": "\ud83d\ude00"}
    expect result = "\"\ud83d\ude00\""

req JC-004 "Numbers"
  decision D-001
  text
    A number MUST be written as ECMAScript's `Number::toString` writes it (edge
    number-text/ecmascript), whatever spelling the request used; `-0` is written `0`.
  example canonicalize {"value": [333333333.33333329, 1E30, 4.50, 2e-3, 0.000000000000000000000000001]}
    expect result = "[333333333.3333333,1e+30,4.5,0.002,1e-27]"
  example canonicalize {"value": [100, 1e2, 1.0e2, 100.000]}
    expect result = "[100,100,100,100]"
  example canonicalize {"value": -0}
    expect result = "0"
  example canonicalize {"value": [1e20, 1e21, 0.000001, 1e-7, -1.5]}
    expect result = "[100000000000000000000,1e+21,0.000001,1e-7,-1.5]"
  example canonicalize {"value": 9007199254740993}
    expect result = "9007199254740992"

req JC-005 "Arrays"
  decision D-001
  text
    An array MUST be written as `[`, its elements' canonical texts in their order separated by
    `,`, then `]`, with no whitespace.
  example canonicalize {"value": [3, 1, 2, [], [{}], ""]}
    expect result = "[3,1,2,[],[{}],\"\"]"

req JC-006 "Objects"
  decision D-001
  text
    An object MUST be written as `{`, its members separated by `,`, then `}`, each member as the
    name's string text, `:`, and the value's canonical text, with no whitespace. Members MUST be
    in ascending order of their names compared as sequences of UTF-16 code units (a shorter name
    that is a prefix of a longer one comes first). Nested objects are ordered the same way.
  example canonicalize {"value": {"b": 1, "a": {"d": 2, "c": [3]}}}
    expect result = "{\"a\":{\"c\":[3],\"d\":2},\"b\":1}"
  example canonicalize {"value": {"ab": 1, "a": 2, "": 3, "B": 4}}
    expect result = "{\"\":3,\"B\":4,\"a\":2,\"ab\":1}"
  example canonicalize {"value": {"\u20ac": "Euro Sign", "\r": "Carriage Return", "\ufb33": "Hebrew Letter Dalet With Dagesh", "1": "One", "\ud83d\ude00": "Emoji: Grinning Face", "\u0080": "Control", "\u00f6": "Latin Small Letter O With Diaeresis"}}
    expect result = "{\"\\r\":\"Carriage Return\",\"1\":\"One\",\"\u0080\":\"Control\",\"\u00f6\":\"Latin Small Letter O With Diaeresis\",\"\u20ac\":\"Euro Sign\",\"\ud83d\ude00\":\"Emoji: Grinning Face\",\"\ufb33\":\"Hebrew Letter Dalet With Dagesh\"}"
  example canonicalize {"value": {"__proto__": 1, "a": 2}}
    expect result = "{\"__proto__\":1,\"a\":2}"
  example canonicalize {"value": {"\\": 1, "\"": 2}}
    expect result = "{\"\\\"\":2,\"\\\\\":1}"

req JC-007 "Bytes"
  decision D-005
  text
    `canonicalBytes` MUST answer the UTF-8 encoding (RFC 8785 section 3.2.4) of the text that
    `canonicalize` gives for the same input, as lowercase hex, two digits a byte. A valid
    surrogate pair is one character, encoded in four bytes.
  example canonicalBytes {"value": "\u20ac"}
    expect result = "22e282ac22"
  example canonicalBytes {"value": {"\ud83d\ude00": 1, "\u00e9": 2}}
    expect result = "7b22c3a9223a322c22f09f9880223a317d"
  example canonicalBytes {"value": {"numbers": [333333333.33333329, 1E30, 4.50, 2e-3, 0.000000000000000000000000001], "string": "\u20ac$\u000F\u000aA'\u0042\u0022\u005c\\\"\/", "literals": [null, true, false]}}
    expect result = "7b226c69746572616c73223a5b6e756c6c2c747275652c66616c73655d2c226e756d62657273223a5b3333333333333333332e333333333333332c31652b33302c342e352c302e3030322c31652d32375d2c22737472696e67223a22e282ac245c75303030665c6e4127425c225c5c5c5c5c222f227d"

req JC-008 "The strict statement form"
  decision D-003, D-004
  text
    With `strict` true, a value MUST be refused with `no_canonical_form` when it holds a boolean,
    or a number that is not an integer from 0 to 2^53 − 1 (9007199254740991) inclusive. Every
    other value is written exactly as without `strict`. `strict` absent means false.
  example canonicalize {"value": {"b": 9007199254740991, "a": [null, "x"]}, "strict": true}
    expect result = "{\"a\":[null,\"x\"],\"b\":9007199254740991}"
  example canonicalize {"value": 1.5, "strict": true}
    expect error = "no_canonical_form"
  example canonicalize {"value": 1.5}
    expect result = "1.5"
  example canonicalize {"value": 9007199254740992, "strict": true}
    expect error = "no_canonical_form"
  example canonicalize {"value": -1, "strict": true}
    expect error = "no_canonical_form"
  example canonicalize {"value": {"a": [false]}, "strict": true}
    expect error = "no_canonical_form"
  example canonicalBytes {"value": true, "strict": true}
    expect error = "no_canonical_form"
  example canonicalize {"value": [0, 1.0, 1e2], "strict": true}
    expect result = "[0,1,100]"
  example canonicalize {"value": -0, "strict": true}
    expect result = "0"

req JC-009 "Recognising canonical text"
  decision D-003
  text
    `isCanonical` MUST answer true exactly when `text` is one JSON text whose value has a
    canonical form (in the strict form when `strict` is true) and `text` equals that canonical
    form character for character. Text that is not JSON, that has whitespace outside strings or
    anything around the value, or that repeats a member name, is not canonical.
  table isCanonical
    | text                    | strict | result |
    | "{\"a\":1,\"b\":2}"     |        | true   |
    | "{\"b\":2,\"a\":1}"     |        | false  |
    | "{\"a\": 1}"            |        | false  |
    | " {\"a\":1}"            |        | false  |
    | "{\"a\":1}\n"           |        | false  |
    | "{\"a\":1.0}"           |        | false  |
    | "{\"a\":\"\\u0041\"}"   |        | false  |
    | "\"\\u001f\""           |        | true   |
    | "\"\\u001F\""           |        | false  |
    | "\"\\/\""               |        | false  |
    | "\"/\""                 |        | true   |
    | "\"\\ud800\""           |        | false  |
    | "1e+21"                 |        | true   |
    | "1E+21"                 |        | false  |
    | "1e21"                  |        | false  |
    | "-0"                    |        | false  |
    | "0"                     |        | true   |
    | "null"                  |        | true   |
    | ""                      |        | false  |
    | "nul"                   |        | false  |
    | "{\"a\":1,\"a\":1}"     |        | false  |
    | "{\"\\\\\":1}"          |        | true   |
    | "{\"\":0,\"\\\\\":0}"   |        | true   |
    | "{\"\":0,\"\\\"\":0}"   |        | true   |
    | "{\"a\":true}"          |        | true   |
    | "{\"a\":true}"          | true   | false  |
    | "9007199254740991"      | true   | true   |
    | "9007199254740992"      | true   | false  |

open JC-010 "Values outside JSON"
  text
    NaN, the infinities, `undefined` members and other values that a JSON request cannot carry:
    the library refuses non-finite numbers and leaves out undefined members, but no request can
    reach either.

open JC-011 "Depth"
  text
    How deeply nested a value may be before an implementation gives up, and what it answers then.

open JC-012 "Which part is refused"
  text
    When a value has several parts with no canonical form, which one an implementation reports;
    only the code `no_canonical_form` is pinned.

property JC-P1 "The canonical text reads back as the value and is canonical"
  supports JC-004, JC-006, JC-009
  for v in json
  call a = canonicalize {"value": v}
  call b = isCanonical {"text": a.result}
  call c = canonicalize {"value": parse(a.result)}
  expect parse(a.result) == v
  expect b.result == true
  expect c.result == a.result

property JC-P2 "Strict mode refuses or agrees"
  supports JC-008
  for v in json
  call s = canonicalize {"value": v, "strict": true}
  call n = canonicalize {"value": v}
  expect has(s, "error") or s.result == n.result

property JC-P3 "Bytes are never fewer than characters"
  supports JC-007
  for v in json
  call t = canonicalize {"value": v}
  call h = canonicalBytes {"value": v}
  expect len(h.result) >= 2 * len(t.result)

evidence JC-NUMBERS "RFC 8785 Appendix B number samples"
  source RFC 8785, Appendix B (https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8785.txt), as copied into x402-solidarity's conformance/jcs-rfc8785.json at d6653c7
  kind published
  supports JC-004
  text
    The RFC gives each sample as a binary64 bit pattern and its text. A request cannot carry a
    bit pattern, so each row's input is a decimal that names that same binary64 value: the
    expected text itself (ECMAScript's text always reads back as the same value), except
    0x4430000000000000, written as the exact integer 2^68. The two non-finite rows cannot be
    sent and are left out.
  table canonicalize
    | value                     | result                       |
    | 0                         | "0"                          |
    | -0                        | "0"                          |
    | 5e-324                    | "5e-324"                     |
    | -5e-324                   | "-5e-324"                    |
    | 1.7976931348623157e308    | "1.7976931348623157e+308"    |
    | -1.7976931348623157e308   | "-1.7976931348623157e+308"   |
    | 9007199254740992          | "9007199254740992"           |
    | -9007199254740992         | "-9007199254740992"          |
    | 295147905179352825856     | "295147905179352830000"      |
    | 9.999999999999997e22      | "9.999999999999997e+22"      |
    | 1e23                      | "1e+23"                      |
    | 1.0000000000000001e23     | "1.0000000000000001e+23"     |
    | 999999999999999700000     | "999999999999999700000"      |
    | 999999999999999900000     | "999999999999999900000"      |
    | 1e21                      | "1e+21"                      |
    | 9.999999999999997e-7      | "9.999999999999997e-7"       |
    | 0.000001                  | "0.000001"                   |
    | 333333333.3333332         | "333333333.3333332"          |
    | 333333333.33333325        | "333333333.33333325"         |
    | 333333333.3333333         | "333333333.3333333"          |
    | 333333333.3333334         | "333333333.3333334"          |
    | 333333333.33333343        | "333333333.33333343"         |
    | -0.0000033333333333333333 | "-0.0000033333333333333333"  |
    | 1424953923781206.2        | "1424953923781206.2"         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
decision D-001 "RFC 8785 is the rule for canonical text"
  source inferred from jcs.ts's doc comment ("JSON Canonicalization Scheme, RFC 8785") and its callers, which hash and sign the text (ledger.ts statementBytes, policy.ts policyHash)
  status inferred
  text
    The module exists so that a statement or a policy has one byte sequence to hash and sign.
    Decision: follow RFC 8785 section 3.2 as written (ECMAScript string and number text, UTF-16
    member order, UTF-8 bytes). Why: other parties check the signatures, and RFC 8785 is the
    rule they can implement without this code.
  rejected "Sorting member names by code point or by UTF-8 bytes: RFC 8785 3.2.3 orders by UTF-16 code units, and the two differ for names above U+FFFF."

decision D-002 "Unpaired surrogates are refused, not escaped"
  source inferred from jcs.ts's doc comment ("Lone surrogates are rejected, because the input must be I-JSON (RFC 7493)")
  status inferred
  text
    ECMAScript's JSON.stringify escapes an unpaired surrogate as `\udXXX`, which yields text, but
    RFC 8785 takes I-JSON input, which excludes unpaired surrogates. Decision: no canonical form
    (edge json/rfc8785, not json/sorted-utf16). Why: a value that two parties may encode
    differently into UTF-8 has no single byte sequence to sign.
  rejected "Escaping them as json/sorted-utf16 does: the text would exist, but its UTF-8 bytes would not be well-formed for every reader."

decision D-003 "The strict form is the statement form"
  source inferred from jcs.ts's doc comment ("Statements are limited by the spec to strings, integers below 2^53, arrays, objects and null") and ledger.ts, which reads a statement payload with isCanonical in strict mode
  status inferred
  text
    The ledger signs statements whose numbers are amounts and counts. Decision: strict mode
    refuses booleans and every number that is not a whole number from 0 to 2^53 − 1, and
    `isCanonical` in strict mode answers false for such text. Why: amounts and counts are never
    negative or fractional, and above 2^53 − 1 binary64 no longer holds every integer.

decision D-004 "Negative zero is zero in the strict form"
  source inferred: jcs.ts says "non-negative integers" and "-0 serializes as 0"; neither says what strict mode does with -0
  status inferred
  text
    A request can carry `-0`. It is an integer and is not negative (it compares equal to 0), and
    its canonical text is `0`. Decision: strict mode accepts it and writes `0`. Why: refusing it
    would make the strict form depend on a sign that the canonical text does not show.
  rejected "Refusing -0 in strict mode: the canonical text, 0, would then be refused for one spelling of the value and accepted for another."

decision D-005 "Bytes travel as lowercase hex"
  source proposed for this record: the library returns a Uint8Array, which the driver protocol cannot carry
  status proposed
  text
    The driver protocol carries JSON, so `canonicalBytes` answers the bytes as lowercase hex,
    two digits a byte. Why: the same form as RFC 8785's own samples and the module's tests.

decision D-006 "One error code for every value with no canonical form"
  source proposed for this record: the library throws an Error whose message varies by cause
  status proposed
  text
    The library throws for an unpaired surrogate, a non-finite number, and (strict) a boolean or
    a number outside the statement form, with different messages. Decision: the driver answers
    all of these `no_canonical_form`, and which part is reported is left open (OPEN-JC-012).
    Why: a caller acts on "this value cannot be signed", not on which part failed first.
